But 8^7, let me write that as (2^3)^7 = 2^{21}, so yes, \( 2^{21} = 2^{21} \), that checks out.

Second equation original: \( 9^{y} = 3^{x - 9} \)

With y = 6 and x = 21,

Left-hand side: \( 9^{6} \)

Right-hand side: \( 3^{21 - 9} = 3^{12} \)

9^6 can be written as (3^2)^6 = 3^{12}, which also gives \( 3^{12} \), so that matches as well.

Therefore, yes, both equations hold true with x = 21 and y = 6.

Thus, x + y = 21 + 6 = 27, which is option (D).

But let me see if perhaps I made any error in manipulation.

